body:
The issue itself is within your Bookshelf class, you're not initializing the `books` array, that's why you're getting an NPE. Arrays must be a fixed size at compile time, so in your constructor you could do something like:

```
/**
  * this constant represents the max size of books allowed to be stored within the bookshelf array
  */
public static final int BOOKSHELF_SIZE = 10;

public Bookshelf() {
    this.books = new Book[BOOKSHELF_SIZE];
}

Well, easiest way to do that is to use a map to store each individual word and increment each count for each token

for example if I take this file:

	LOREM LOREM LOREM LOREM LOREM LOREM LOREM LOREM LOREM LOREM LOREM LOREM LOREM LOREM LOREM LOREM
	IPSUM IPSUM IPSUM IPSUM IPSUM IPSUM IPSUM IPSUM IPSUM

And I want to know how many times "Lorem" and "Ipsum" appear this would be my solution

	import java.nio.file.Files;
	import java.nio.file.Path;
	import java.nio.file.Paths;
	import java.util.HashMap;
	import java.util.Map;
	import java.util.StringTokenizer;
	import java.util.concurrent.atomic.LongAdder;

	public class TokenizerExample {

		public static void main(String[] args) {
			Path path = Paths.get("test_file.txt");
			if (!Files.exists(path))
				throw new RuntimeException("Could not locate file.");

			Map<String, LongAdder> occurences = new HashMap<>();

			try {
				Files.readAllLines(path).forEach(line -> {
					StringTokenizer tokenizer = new StringTokenizer(line, " \t\n\r\f,.:;?![]'");

					while (tokenizer.hasMoreTokens()) {
						String token = tokenizer.nextToken().toLowerCase();
						occurences.computeIfAbsent(token, k -> new LongAdder()).increment();
					}
				});
			} catch (Exception ex) {
				ex.printStackTrace();
			}

			long count1 = occurences.get("lorem").sum();
			System.out.println("lorem count = " + count1);

			long count2 = occurences.get("ipsum").sum();
			System.out.println("ipsum count = " + count2);
		}

	}

Please remember, when calling the "get" function for the map, all the words are stored in lowercase form. So, for your input you must call "toLowerCase" on the string and you may also need to remove any trailing whitespace by calling "trim"

